WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.720
Hi everyone, my name is Gabby Moreno&nbsp;
Cesar and I'm with the IBM Cloud team.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.880
In this video we'll show you how to run batch&nbsp;
jobs with IBM Cloud Code Engine. Batch jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.800
are pieces of code meant to perform specific&nbsp;
tasks at certain times of day and then stop -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.240
for example, processing daily&nbsp;
records in a back-end system.  
 &nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.440
So, starting at the Code Engine "overview"&nbsp;
page, we are going to choose the "Run your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:29.760
container image" option and give it the name&nbsp;
of a "batch" container image: "hmo-task" 
 &nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.520
To save time, this image has&nbsp;
already been built for us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.080
Let's go ahead and hit "Start creating"&nbsp;
to move on to the next step. 
 &nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.840
On this screen we can enter many different&nbsp;
configuration options but for now let's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.960
stick with the defaults for everything except&nbsp;
for the first one... for that we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:58.160
to choose "Job" instead of "Application". 
 
With that let's go ahead and hit "Create" 
 &nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:04.480
We have now created the definition of our Batch&nbsp;
Job. Meaning, we didn't actually run it yet - we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:12.800
just made the platform aware of our Job. This&nbsp;
will allow us to run it over and over as needed. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:18.240
Before we actually run the job, let's&nbsp;
first talk about what the job will do. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:27.040
For this demo we have an application that manages&nbsp;
a set of customer records and our Job will process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.880
all of the customer records in our&nbsp;
backend system. You don't need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.360
understand what the records are for, or what&nbsp;
this front-end application is that I'm showing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:41.440
The key things here are the two numbers that&nbsp;
are from our backend system. The first shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:47.200
the number of records in there, and the second&nbsp;
shows how many have been processed. As the batch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.640
job runs, the second number will increase and&nbsp;
eventually should equal the first number. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:56.800
Let's just keep this off to&nbsp;
the side so we can watch it. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.080
Let's now run the batch job by&nbsp;
hitting the "Submit job" button. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.000
You'll notice there are a few&nbsp;
options that we can modify. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.320
First, and foremost, is the 'Array indices' field.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:16.080
This tells Code Engine how many instances of the&nbsp;
job to execute. It's showing a default value of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.440
"1" - but this demo we're going to run 10&nbsp;
of them so we're going to enter "1-10" 
 &nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.840
Code Engine allows for you to not only&nbsp;
specify how many instances to run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:34.400
but how those instances are numbered. Meaning,&nbsp;
are they numbered 1, 2, 3... or should they be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:43.680
numbered 1,3,5,7, ... if we only want odd numbered&nbsp;
instances? Often the instance number is used a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.360
part of the processing to ensure each instance's&nbsp;
work doesn't overlap with any other's. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:54.320
We're going to leave the rest of the configuration&nbsp;
options alone and just press "Submit job". 
 &nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.760
Notice in the window that we&nbsp;
have 10 pending instances. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:07.480
Each instance will eventually move from "pending",&nbsp;
to "running" and finally "completed" states 
 &nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:14.400
Notice in the application window you can see the&nbsp;
number of records processed is going up... fast. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.040
And in a matter of seconds&nbsp;
all 10 jobs are completed 
 &nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:26.160
Let's quickly summarize what happen though. With&nbsp;
just the name of a container image we defined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.840
a Job and then executed it with 10 instances.&nbsp;
We didn't have to do any other infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:39.120
setup or management. As a developer of this code,&nbsp;
all I had to worry about is the business logic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.560
workload - nothing else. 
 
And that's the goal of Code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.320
Engine - allowing developers to focus&nbsp;
on what's important... coding. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.560
That's all the time we have today, but hopefully&nbsp;
you found this quick walk-thru of how to manage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.680
batch jobs in Code Engine exciting and I encourage&nbsp;
you to look at our other videos to see more key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:02.320
Code Engine features. And, of course, we hope that&nbsp;
you'll go to www.ibm.com/cloud/code-engine to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.000
with it yourself. 
 
Thank you.

